(* Go+ v0.18.0 grammar delta: a spelled proof no longer forces spelled
   indices, and an unresolved skeleton is never written.

   The complete base grammar is Go plus the declarations and expressions in
   grammar-v0.17.0.ebnf. No production changes. This file records the two
   contract changes above it.
*)

DependentCall   = FunctionName "(" [ ArgumentList ] ")" .

(*
  ## An index can be inferred; a proof cannot

  A dependent signature mixes two kinds of erased parameter. An INDEX is
  recoverable from the arguments — `n` in `v Vec[T, n]` is whatever `v`'s
  own type says. A PROOF is not: it is a claim about those indices, and
  v0.14.0 made writing it mandatory precisely because nothing can supply
  it on the author's behalf.

  Erased arguments were nevertheless omitted as a GROUP. A call named all
  of them or none, so naming the mandatory proof forced spelling every
  index beside it:

      return AtIndex(i-1, n-1, decide, t)   (* before *)
      return AtIndex(i-1, decide, t)        (* now *)

  A call may now omit exactly the inferable erased arguments and still
  name its proofs. The older shape — every erased argument omitted —
  remains valid wherever no proof is carried, so the 78 existing
  omitted-index sites are unaffected, and the fully-spelled shape is
  unchanged.

  - Nothing is waved through. The index is inferred, then the proposition
    is checked against it: `AtIndex(7, decide, v)` on a `Vec[int, 3]` is
    still `cannot prove 7 < 3`, and omitting `decide` is still
    `the proof argument for p of AtIndex cannot be omitted`.
  - Inference now binds what it can rather than abandoning a whole
    instantiation at the first position it cannot solve. Unifying the
    parameter's `Vec[T, n]` against the caller's `Vec[int, 3]` clashes at
    `T` — a type parameter, not a dependent variable — and used to give up
    there, losing `n`, the one position inference wanted. It could only
    ever succeed when the caller's type spelled the callee's own type
    parameters, which is true of a recursive step and false of a call site.
  - std/vec's `AtIndex` and `Set` are written in the shorter form. The
    generated Go is byte-identical: erased is erased either way.

  ## An unresolved skeleton is not an artifact

  Pass 1 does not lower a construct to Go. It lowers it to a skeleton plus
  a carrier that resolution consumes: a match becomes `case nil:` heads
  whose bodies are `//goplus:pattern` comments, a pipeline becomes a
  `__gp_bare_` call, an expression-position block becomes a `__gp_val`
  binding. Resolution needs type information, so it needs a module.

  Without one it did not run, and the skeleton was WRITTEN OUT as if it
  were the finished artifact — two `case nil:` arms in one switch, binders
  that were never bound, no return — from a command that exited 0. The
  file parses as Go, which is why it went unnoticed: three goml scenarios
  and four goml tests had been asserting against it.

  Generation now checks the artifact for those carriers before writing,
  and refuses:

      a match cannot be generated here: this package has no module
      context, so it cannot be resolved; run inside a module

  This is the rule typed holes already followed (v0.10.0), one step more
  general. It is keyed off the reserved `__gp_` names and the pattern
  marker rather than the skeleton's shape, because `case nil:` is a legal
  arm of a real Go type switch and std/decimal writes one. Go+ that needs
  no resolution still generates outside a module, so a single `.gp` file
  remains usable as a script.
*)
